{
  "example": "`fn[load]` moves unchanged from `src/config.rs` to `src/config/load.rs`, and\nnothing else changes. `habeas refactor --map 'src/config.rs#fn[load]=src/config/load.rs#fn[load]'`\nrewrites the outline inputs that read it, recomputes the reference-set digests\nof the requirements it implements, rewrites `` `src/config.rs#fn[load]` `` in\ntheir rationales, and validation then passes without calling a model.\n{cite}`requirements.refactor.outline_inputs`\n{cite}`requirements.refactor.reference_digest`\n{cite}`requirements.refactor.rationale.addresses`\n{cite}`requirements.refactor.equivalence`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The example describes a concrete case: `fn[load]` moves unchanged from `src/config.rs` to `src/config/load.rs`, and `habeas refactor --map ...` is run. It states four outcomes: the outline inputs are rewritten, the reference-set digests are recomputed, the rationale addresses are rewritten, and validation passes without a model. `tests/refactor.rs#fn[a_moved_function_keeps_its_evidence_current]` carries the `{test}` reference to it. I read its helpers (`project`, `config_source`, `write_evidence`, `author`, `move_load`, `errors`, `snapshot`, `LOAD_ALONE`).\n\n**Setup.**\n- `project` and `config_source` build a fixture where `src/config.rs` holds `load`, `check` and `verify`. `load` carries `{impl}rules.load`, and `verify` carries `{test}` references to both requirements.\n- `author` writes current evidence for `rules.load` and `rules.check`. Each record has a real reference-set digest from `habeas::reference_set_digest_for` and real outline inputs from `habeas::outline_input`.\n- The `rules.load` rationale names `` `src/config.rs#fn[load]` `` and also mentions `verify` and `src/config.rs` separately.\n- `move_load` moves `load` verbatim, with its `{impl}` marker, into `src/config/load.rs` and leaves the rest in `src/config.rs`. That is the unchanged move the example describes.\n\n**Outcomes checked.**\n- **Before the refactor:** the test asserts validation is clean after `author`. It asserts that after the move, the errors mention `fn[load]`. Validation is therefore stale before the refactor, so a pass afterwards cannot be vacuous.\n- **Outline inputs rewritten:** after the refactor the test asserts `record[\"inputs\"][1][\"file\"] == \"src/config/load.rs\"`. The `fn[load]` input hash must also match the mapped-back text for validation to pass.\n- **Digest recomputed:** the `impl` for `rules.load` now sits in `src/config/load.rs`, so the reference set has changed. `errors(root)` is asserted empty after the refactor. That can only hold if the `references` digest was rewritten, because the digest is part of validation and nothing else touches the record. This also checks `requirements.refactor.reference_digest` and `requirements.refactor.equivalence`.\n- **Rationale address rewritten:** the test asserts the exact rationale string. `` `src/config/load.rs#fn[load]` `` is rewritten. The non-address mention `` `src/config.rs` `` is left alone and reported for review, which the test also asserts. This checks `requirements.refactor.rationale.addresses`.\n- **Validation passes without a model:** `errors` calls `habeas::validate_repository` and nothing else. No model call is involved in the test's validation or refactor, so a pass shows validation passes without one.\n- **Nothing else changes:** the test asserts the report lists only `evidence/rules.load.json` (`Changed 1 records`) and does not mention `rules.check`. It asserts the `rules.check` file bytes are unchanged. It compares a full-tree `snapshot` before and after, excluding only the `rules.load` record. A second run reports `Changed 0 records`.\n\nI found no gap against the example's text.",
